  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nBroad, upper-level decent behind a passing shortwave trough has allowed \nclear skies and dry conditions to develop over the area this morning. \nThese clear skies, along with weak westerly winds around 5-10 MPH, will \nallow highs to climb into the 60s this afternoon. These clear skies \nwill be interrupted by an upper-level cloud band moving over the area \nthis evening through tonight. Despite the temporary increase in cloud \ncover, temperatures are expected to fall into the upper 30s to low 40s \nby tomorrow morning. These cool temperatures along with light winds \ngradually becoming more northerly promote the development of fog \ntonight into tomorrow morning, particularly south of Interstate 80 in \nlow-lying areas. The latest 2 REFS ensemble runs (6Z and 12Z) both show \na 20-40% chance of seeing visibilities dropping below 1 mile by \ntomorrow morning. \n\nAs the upper-level trough continues to progress eastward, a small ridge \nis expected to build into the area Sunday through Monday. This ridge \nwill help promote the persisting dry conditions, clear skies, and high \ntemperatures increasing into the mid 60s Sunday and Tuesday afternoons \nand into the mid to upper 60s Monday afternoon. Light northerly winds \nSunday morning will gradually become southerly throughout the day, with \nspeeds remaining around 5-10 MPH. \n\nThe chance of fog returns tomorrow night into Monday morning, as light \nwinds and clear skies allow lows to fall into the low 40s. Recent model \nguidance currently indicates a 40-70% of seeing visibilities falling \nbelow 1 mile by Monday at 8AM, particularly for areas south of Highway \n30. \n\nOverall, model guidance is in agreement that a shortwave trough will \npass over the area Tuesday evening into Wednesday, causing surface \npressure to fall across the area. This strengthening pressure \ngradient will cause the surface wind speeds to increase, with \ngusts of 30-35 MPH possible Wednesday. Despite this wave moving \noverhead, the chance of precipitation remains minimal (up to a \n5% chance Wednesday).\n\nAs a larger scale trough, with embedded shortwaves, develops across the \nnortheast and a ridge develops over the western CONUS, greater \nuncertainty enters the forecast, as models disagree on the location and \nmagnitude of this large-scale wave. For example, current ECMWF guidance \nshifts this trough more to the east, allowing warmer temperatures to \ndevelop across the Plains towards the end of next week. On the \ncontrary, the GFS has this trough shifted more to the west, keeping \ncooler temperatures and windy conditions in the forecast longer. This \ndisagreement has lead to current NBM guidance showing a spread of 15 \ndegrees between the 75th to 25th percentile Friday afternoon, with a \nspread of 10 degrees entering the area as early as Thursday\nmorning.",
